Running PtokaX under systemd
============================

Files here
----------

  ptokax@.service.in           templated unit, with version guards
  ptokax@.socket.in            optional: systemd owns the hub's listening sockets
  ptokax-console@.socket.in    optional: per-instance Lua console socket
  unitgen.sh                   renders the template for a target systemd version
  ptokax.target                group handle for every instance
  pxctl                        admin front-end
  ptokax.sysusers.example      optional static user, one per instance
  group-access.conf.example    drop-in: group access instead of a transient UID
  no-capabilities.conf.example drop-in: drop CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
  console-access.conf.example  drop-in: reach the Lua console without sudo
  pxconsole                    socat client for the console directives
  test-console.sh              exercises the console against a transient instance

Install
-------

  ./configure --prefix=/usr && make
  sudo make install                          # binary
  sudo make install-systemd                  # unit, target, pxctl   [Phase 2]
  sudo systemctl daemon-reload

Until the install-systemd target lands, render and place the unit by hand:

  ./contrib/systemd/unitgen.sh \
      --systemd-version="$(systemctl --version | awk 'NR==1{print $2}')" \
      --define=bindir=/usr/bin \
      --define=sysconfdir=/etc \
      --define=docdir=/usr/share/doc/ptokax \
      < contrib/systemd/ptokax@.service.in \
      | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/'ptokax@.service' >/dev/null
  sudo systemctl daemon-reload

pxctl carries the same @bindir@/@sysconfdir@/@datadir@/@docdir@ placeholders and is
substituted by the same install target. Running it from the source tree before then,
override the paths in the environment:

  PTOKAX_BIN=/usr/bin/PtokaX ./contrib/systemd/pxctl list

push and pull shell out to rsync. Nothing else in pxctl or the hub needs it, so
configure only warns when it is missing and push and pull check again at run time.

The unit needs systemd 240 or newer for Type=exec; older systemd gets Type=simple, which
cannot report a failed start. Directives added after 240 are dropped automatically for
older targets, so the rendered unit is always valid -- just less hardened.

Instances
---------

One hub per instance name:

  sudo pxctl create main
  sudo pxctl setup main          # configuration menu; hub must be stopped
  sudo systemctl enable --now ptokax@main

  pxctl list
  pxctl log main -f
  systemctl stop ptokax.target       # every instance at once

Each instance gets its own state directory and its own UID. Instance A cannot read
instance B's files -- which matters, because cfg/Settings.pxt stores PostgresPass and
MySQLPass in plaintext.

Identity: three modes
---------------------

The unit sets `User=ptokax-%i` together with `DynamicUser=yes`. systemd uses a static
user of that name if one exists, and otherwise allocates a transient UID. That gives
three modes without changing the shipped unit.

  A. Default. No setup. Transient UID per instance, released on stop. State lives at
     /var/lib/ptokax/<instance>, which is a symlink into /var/lib/private -- mode 0700
     root, so inspecting it needs sudo.

  B. Stable UID. Create a static ptokax-<instance> user (ptokax.sysusers.example).
     The UID is then reserved rather than drawn from the transient pool, so it cannot
     be handed to a different service later. Note this does NOT change what the host
     sees: where the kernel supports id-mapped mounts, systemd owns the state directory
     as `nobody` (65534) on the host and maps it to the service's UID inside, whether
     or not the static user exists. Verified on systemd 261 / kernel 7.1 -- both a
     transient and a static instance showed 65534 on the host.

     For backup, that means tar the directory as root and restore it as root; the
     `nobody` ownership is what systemd expects to find. Do not "fix" it to a real UID.

  C. Group access. Mode B plus group-access.conf.example, which turns DynamicUser off.
     State moves to /var/lib/ptokax/<instance> at mode 0750, so members of the
     ptokax-<instance> group can read logs and drop .lua files in without sudo. This is
     the only mode that gives that; it costs the implied hardening DynamicUser brings.

The Lua console socket is not part of this. systemd creates it as root before the
service starts, so SocketGroup= in console-access.conf.example names any group you
like and has no bearing on the service's User=. Mode A keeps all its hardening with
an unprivileged console.

Port 411
--------

411 is the standard NMDC port and is privileged. The unit grants
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, which is scoped to the service rather than to
the binary as `setcap` would be.

Be aware that ambient capabilities are inherited across execve, and PtokaX exposes the
full Lua standard library including os.execute and io.popen. A child process spawned by
a hub script therefore holds CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE. If you run untrusted scripts, move
every port above 1023 and apply no-capabilities.conf.example.

The alternative sometimes suggested -- `sysctl net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=411`
-- is not recommended. It applies to the whole host, and it opens not just 411 but every
port from 411 to 1023 to every local process, 443 and 993 included.

Several hubs on port 411
------------------------

The kernel binds (address, port), not port, so N hubs can each serve 411 given N
addresses. IPv6 makes that cheap -- a /64 is more addresses than you will ever need.

Sharing one address is not possible. The hub sends $Lock before the client sends
anything, so there is nothing for a proxy to route on; no SNI equivalent exists.

Two ways to give an instance its own address:

  - Set BindOnlySingleIP in the instance's settings. The bound address is then taken
    from the resolved HubAddress, so this only works when the address you advertise is
    the address you bind -- not the case behind NAT.

  - Socket activation, below. ListenStream= names the bind address directly and leaves
    HubAddress free, which is the general answer.

Socket activation
-----------------

systemd binds the sockets and passes them to the hub, so the hub needs no capability:

  systemctl enable --now ptokax@main.socket
  # plus no-capabilities.conf.example as a service drop-in

Adoption is additive. The hub uses a passed socket when one matches a port in its
TCPPorts setting, and binds any remaining port itself. That keeps the settings file
authoritative and makes a disagreement visible rather than silent:

  - a passed socket the hub does not want is logged at warning level
  - a configured port the hub had to bind itself, and could not, is logged as an error

ListenStream= cannot use %i, so per-instance ports go in a drop-in -- see the comments
in ptokax@.socket.

Editing configuration
---------------------

PtokaX holds its settings in memory and rewrites cfg/ on every stop. Editing
cfg/Settings.pxt under a running hub loses the edit when the hub next shuts down.

  - To change settings: stop the instance, then `pxctl setup <instance>`.
  - `pxctl setup` refuses to run against an active instance for this reason.

Logs
----

  pxctl log main -f
  journalctl PTOKAX_INSTANCE=main -p err

Note that at present the hub still writes its own files under the state directory's
logs/ and little reaches the journal beyond startup output. The logging rework that
routes everything through the journal is tracked in ../../init_hitlist.

Deliberate omissions
--------------------

  ProcSubset=pid          hides /proc/cpuinfo (CPU count) and /proc/net/arp, which
                          backs a Lua-visible GetMacAddress call
  PrivateUsers=yes        silently defeats CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
  WatchdogSec=            the main loop can block inside a Lua handler or during the
                          shutdown save; a watchdog would SIGABRT mid-write
  network-online.target   a server that only accepts connections does not need it

MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes is safe for PUC Lua but breaks LuaJIT. If you built against
LuaJIT, override it with a drop-in.

Troubleshooting
---------------

status=203/EXEC, "Unable to locate executable"
    The service runs with PrivateTmp=yes and therefore has its own /tmp. A binary
    under /tmp is invisible to it even though it exists on the host. Install the
    binary somewhere real.

The hub starts but binds nothing
    Start() only fails outright when no port binds at all. A partial failure -- 411
    refused while 1209 succeeded -- leaves the hub running. Check the journal.

Verifying a change to the unit
------------------------------

  systemd-analyze verify contrib/systemd/ptokax@.service
  systemd-analyze security --offline=true --threshold=20 contrib/systemd/ptokax@.service

The rendered unit currently scores 1.6 with every assessment passing.

Mind the two scales: the exposure level is printed as 0.0-10.0, but --threshold= takes
the same number times ten, and exits nonzero when the score exceeds it. A unit scoring
1.6 passes --threshold=16 and fails --threshold=15. The 20 above leaves a little room
for a directive that has to be dropped on an older systemd.
